In article <2609@ucl-cs.uucp> J.Purchase@cs.ucl.ac.uk (Jan Purchase) writes:
>
>
>Having lost yet another character to starvation after his diety
>refused to feed him ("Thou art arrogant mortal!"), I noticed that on
>dying my intrinsic list included:
>
>	You are stridently aligned
>
>I always thought that praying before one's prayer timeout has expired
>alienated one's god, and thus, one's standing in his/her eyes - is
>this not so? Aren't constant requests for divine intervention viewed
>as a violation of alignment?

    Nope.  Alignment has to do with your behavior as correlated with your
status (chaotic, neutral, lawful).  If you're chaotic, and go around
killing everything that moves, sacrificing humans, and acting generally
nasty, you're stridently aligned; it's not dependent on praying.

>A related question: assuming your character's inter-prayer timeout is
>p turns and he/she prays, x turns *before* this timeout. After he/she
>is punished (by losing a level or whatever), how many turns must
>elapse before he/she may pray safely again?

    I don't know this one for sure.  I always wait 1000 moves between
praying, and never get into "too-frequent" trouble. 

